[[:db/retract 17592186172377 :question/answer-fields 17592186169125] {:db/id 17592186172130 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2c7beff7-ff72-566b-ae77-d272cca30605" "question-26a444d6-a870-593f-bb03-1c02941c48b1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171710 :question/problem "![](images/1e/1ef5ddc89d7846d1126c60f34fc400669d23d6ca16c2b622addaf2916955ed27.svg)\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z) = z-y$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u^2, \\: -v, \\: v \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v) \\in D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where $u \\geq 0.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\| = 2\\sqrt{2}u.$*" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172131 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d27e0ee0-ad93-5416-8abb-f21167765a8b" "question-f95319f6-2e43-511b-ae0d-9608a991c217"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-2c7beff7-ff72-566b-ae77-d272cca30605" :question/id #uuid "2c7beff7-ff72-566b-ae77-d272cca30605" :question/math-academy-id "q-161288" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=-x-{z}^{2}$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨{u}^{3},\\,v,\\,{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{u}^{2},\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3{u}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2v\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-6v{u}^{2},\\,3{u}^{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨0,\\,-6v{u}^{2},\\,3{u}^{2}⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(-6v{u}^{2})^{2}+(3{u}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{v}^{2}{u}^{4}+9{u}^{4}} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(-x-{z}^{2})\\,{|}_{x={u}^{3}\\,y=v\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =-{u}^{3}-({v}^{2}\\cdot {v}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{u}^{3}-{v}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,3{u}^{2}(-{u}^{3}-{v}^{4})\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+1}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-72a815fc-9d19-53b2-b0ae-b65abcc40317"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-72a815fc-9d19-53b2-b0ae-b65abcc40317" :answer-field/id #uuid "72a815fc-9d19-53b2-b0ae-b65abcc40317" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6a78c70d-c252-5fa1-b3b9-b5253f7112ea" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6a78c70d-c252-5fa1-b3b9-b5253f7112ea" "answer-a93dd97e-74c6-56c8-a6b1-3cdadc7213e0" "answer-cb42e303-0573-52db-81ce-d57e99e6f761" "answer-d505a7da-72d5-5c19-aa46-75ea67cf0bb1" "answer-0cd71aa2-2da0-53d3-a18e-57cc7dd66fe2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a78c70d-c252-5fa1-b3b9-b5253f7112ea" :answer/id #uuid "6a78c70d-c252-5fa1-b3b9-b5253f7112ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{u}^{2}(-{u}^{3}-{v}^{4})\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a93dd97e-74c6-56c8-a6b1-3cdadc7213e0" :answer/id #uuid "a93dd97e-74c6-56c8-a6b1-3cdadc7213e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{u}^{2}({u}^{4}-{v}^{3})\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}+1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb42e303-0573-52db-81ce-d57e99e6f761" :answer/id #uuid "cb42e303-0573-52db-81ce-d57e99e6f761" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6{v}^{2}({u}^{2}+{v}^{4})\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d505a7da-72d5-5c19-aa46-75ea67cf0bb1" :answer/id #uuid "d505a7da-72d5-5c19-aa46-75ea67cf0bb1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(-{u}^{3}-{v}^{4})\\sqrt{4{v}^{3}+u}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0cd71aa2-2da0-53d3-a18e-57cc7dd66fe2" :answer/id #uuid "0cd71aa2-2da0-53d3-a18e-57cc7dd66fe2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{u}^{2}(-{u}^{3}+{v}^{4})\\sqrt{4uv+u}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-26a444d6-a870-593f-bb03-1c02941c48b1" :question/id #uuid "26a444d6-a870-593f-bb03-1c02941c48b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161124" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,u,\\,v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=u\\,z=v} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+{u}^{2} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2\\sqrt{2}{u}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-16fadbea-4516-5df4-9f64-8b7cc353d5a0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-16fadbea-4516-5df4-9f64-8b7cc353d5a0" :answer-field/id #uuid "16fadbea-4516-5df4-9f64-8b7cc353d5a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b146a4db-c677-57ea-92f8-07f41239596d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b146a4db-c677-57ea-92f8-07f41239596d" "answer-c87217ff-e3d3-5223-ac71-463a0c197918" "answer-b723dafe-f5a6-5a70-be14-953187b88dde" "answer-e4f2c71c-8604-5aa1-9ce7-1663a5f2e6db" "answer-31c23b1e-7cd7-5e7a-8c99-254c33c9229b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b146a4db-c677-57ea-92f8-07f41239596d" :answer/id #uuid "b146a4db-c677-57ea-92f8-07f41239596d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}{u}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c87217ff-e3d3-5223-ac71-463a0c197918" :answer/id #uuid "c87217ff-e3d3-5223-ac71-463a0c197918" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{u}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b723dafe-f5a6-5a70-be14-953187b88dde" :answer/id #uuid "b723dafe-f5a6-5a70-be14-953187b88dde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{u}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4f2c71c-8604-5aa1-9ce7-1663a5f2e6db" :answer/id #uuid "e4f2c71c-8604-5aa1-9ce7-1663a5f2e6db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31c23b1e-7cd7-5e7a-8c99-254c33c9229b" :answer/id #uuid "31c23b1e-7cd7-5e7a-8c99-254c33c9229b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{v}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172377 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(2x+y+z)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2u-v,\\,3u+2,\\,u+2v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{70}.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=\\sqrt{70}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(2x+y+z)\\,{|}_{x=2u-v\\,y=3u+2\\,z=u+2v} \\\\  & =2(2u-v)+(3u+2)+(u+2v) \\\\  & =8u+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(8u+2)\\sqrt{70}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}{∬}_{D}\\,(8u+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8u+2)\\,\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}\\cdot [4{u}^{2}+2u]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [u]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{70}\\cdot 6\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{70}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-be382d30-f02e-5329-bcf7-16808280a34b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-be382d30-f02e-5329-bcf7-16808280a34b" :answer-field/id #uuid "be382d30-f02e-5329-bcf7-16808280a34b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7d00c43b-e899-573d-afa8-6b12ead91cda" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7d00c43b-e899-573d-afa8-6b12ead91cda" "answer-bc8100ab-c718-514d-8f3c-f28643c256a5" "answer-6ed83581-5e32-5a0e-8a25-178bb99cade4" "answer-d24b3aff-8b91-5bf0-8ab1-f276e0780c98" "answer-11345332-1583-5403-b382-1f7db9544110"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d00c43b-e899-573d-afa8-6b12ead91cda" :answer/id #uuid "7d00c43b-e899-573d-afa8-6b12ead91cda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\sqrt{70}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc8100ab-c718-514d-8f3c-f28643c256a5" :answer/id #uuid "bc8100ab-c718-514d-8f3c-f28643c256a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{70}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ed83581-5e32-5a0e-8a25-178bb99cade4" :answer/id #uuid "6ed83581-5e32-5a0e-8a25-178bb99cade4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36\\sqrt{70}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d24b3aff-8b91-5bf0-8ab1-f276e0780c98" :answer/id #uuid "d24b3aff-8b91-5bf0-8ab1-f276e0780c98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{70}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11345332-1583-5403-b382-1f7db9544110" :answer/id #uuid "11345332-1583-5403-b382-1f7db9544110" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{70}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d27e0ee0-ad93-5416-8abb-f21167765a8b" :question/id #uuid "d27e0ee0-ad93-5416-8abb-f21167765a8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161181" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(3x-2y+3z)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2u+v,\\,2u+v,\\,-v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 0}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=2\\sqrt{2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(3x-2y+3z)\\,{|}_{x=2u+v\\,y=2u+v\\,z=-v} \\\\  & =3(2u+v)-2(2u+v)+3(-v) \\\\  & =2(u-v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2(u-v)\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,(u-v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({\\int }_{-1}^{1}u-v\\,\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}-vu]}_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(0-2v)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{[-{v}^{2}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-93abc2ba-af03-5472-9cce-10fe847607a0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-93abc2ba-af03-5472-9cce-10fe847607a0" :answer-field/id #uuid "93abc2ba-af03-5472-9cce-10fe847607a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e1fe1620-65f9-5859-8a82-9a5f97b2fafb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e1fe1620-65f9-5859-8a82-9a5f97b2fafb" "answer-44291a57-8b30-555c-b7e9-95314ffa4f9d" "answer-38973f90-0f85-5e71-8380-79a67500ceed" "answer-22129875-cc14-5ad3-8bba-b26995c05188" "answer-ab922d47-0766-574c-83bb-4e4ccfbb77ca"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1fe1620-65f9-5859-8a82-9a5f97b2fafb" :answer/id #uuid "e1fe1620-65f9-5859-8a82-9a5f97b2fafb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44291a57-8b30-555c-b7e9-95314ffa4f9d" :answer/id #uuid "44291a57-8b30-555c-b7e9-95314ffa4f9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38973f90-0f85-5e71-8380-79a67500ceed" :answer/id #uuid "38973f90-0f85-5e71-8380-79a67500ceed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22129875-cc14-5ad3-8bba-b26995c05188" :answer/id #uuid "22129875-cc14-5ad3-8bba-b26995c05188" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab922d47-0766-574c-83bb-4e4ccfbb77ca" :answer/id #uuid "ab922d47-0766-574c-83bb-4e4ccfbb77ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f95319f6-2e43-511b-ae0d-9608a991c217" :question/id #uuid "f95319f6-2e43-511b-ae0d-9608a991c217" :question/math-academy-id "q-161179" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(x+y+z)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\,3v,\\,2u-v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=3\\sqrt{6}.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=3\\sqrt{6}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(x+y+z)\\,{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=3v\\,z=2u-v} \\\\  & =(u+v)+(3v)+(2u-v) \\\\  & =3u+3v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(3u+3v)\\cdot 3\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,(u+v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({\\int }_{0}^{1}u+v\\,\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+vu]}_{0}^{1}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}+v)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{6}{[\\frac{v}{2}+\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{6}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-831c917a-be0b-5705-bc09-e4bee9b35277"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-831c917a-be0b-5705-bc09-e4bee9b35277" :answer-field/id #uuid "831c917a-be0b-5705-bc09-e4bee9b35277" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6d9b5bd3-6406-550a-9325-8fcec12f33ef" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f2e130f7-33b8-53a2-a94a-0675fb611102" "answer-1b5899eb-77cf-5b34-b910-c93474d97e73" "answer-8846a5c5-d563-5056-8e15-64f5e19db2bb" "answer-6d9b5bd3-6406-550a-9325-8fcec12f33ef" "answer-8d5f84da-ca2b-5738-b498-90f08c5871a9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2e130f7-33b8-53a2-a94a-0675fb611102" :answer/id #uuid "f2e130f7-33b8-53a2-a94a-0675fb611102" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15\\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b5899eb-77cf-5b34-b910-c93474d97e73" :answer/id #uuid "1b5899eb-77cf-5b34-b910-c93474d97e73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8846a5c5-d563-5056-8e15-64f5e19db2bb" :answer/id #uuid "8846a5c5-d563-5056-8e15-64f5e19db2bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d9b5bd3-6406-550a-9325-8fcec12f33ef" :answer/id #uuid "6d9b5bd3-6406-550a-9325-8fcec12f33ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d5f84da-ca2b-5738-b498-90f08c5871a9" :answer/id #uuid "8d5f84da-ca2b-5738-b498-90f08c5871a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173256 :tutorial/content "Consider a continuous scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ and a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ defined for $(u,v) \\in D,$ where $D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2.$ \n\nIf our surface is \"sufficiently smooth,\" we can define the **surface integral** of $f$ over $S.$ Let's discuss how we might construct such an integral.\n\nFor the sake of simplicity, let's assume that our region $D$ is rectangular. We start by creating a partition $P$ of $D$ in the usual way:\n\n$$\nP = \\left\\{u_0, u_1, \\ldots, u_m\\right\\} \\times \\left\\{v_0, v_1, \\ldots, v_n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nOur partition is shown schematically below.\n\n![](images/6a/6a15c7b587503c82a57d0734c5bc5fda990824ad162724236868461cd92e4fbe.png)\n\n \n\nThe area $\\delta A = \\delta u\\delta v$ of the small rectangle highlighted in the diagram corresponds to a particular patch $S_{ij}$ of area $\\delta S_{ij}$ on our surface. \n\n![](images/1a/1a8774b7aa366d3328b6f81cfe7315b7784a1f5783f14c1f19f83a459cef5434.png)\n\n \n\nLet $P_{ij}^*$ be a point on the surface within the patch $S_{ij}.$ We consider the product \n\n$$\nf(P_{ij}^*) \\cdot \\delta S_{ij}.\n$$\n\nIf we compute this product for all patches and add them together, we get the Riemann sum\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n f(P_{ij}^*)\\cdot\\delta S_{ij}.\n$$\n\nTaking the limit as $\\delta u, \\delta v\\to 0$ gives our definition of the surface integral of $f$ over $S,$ which we denote as\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nFrom previous discussions, we know that the area of the small patch can be approximated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\delta S_{ij} \\approx \\| \\mathbf r'_u \\times \\mathbf r'_v\\|\\,\\delta u\\delta v = \\| \\mathbf r'_u \\times \\mathbf r'_v\\|\\,\\delta A\n$$\n\nIt follows that we can calculate our surface integral using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nThis formula is also valid when $D$ is non-rectangular. \n\nLet's see an example of applying this in practice." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173252 :tutorial/content "Consider the surface integral of the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = x^2+y^2+z^2\n$$\n\n \nover the parametric surface $S,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u, \\: 2v, \\: -u \\big\\rangle, \\quad (u,v)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is the unit square in the $uv$-plane given by\n\n$$\nD = \\{(u,v) \\: | \\: 0\\leq u\\leq 1,\\: 0\\leq v\\leq 1\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the surface integral of $f$ over $S.$\n\nFor a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the surface integral of $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the fundamental vector product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨2,\\,0,\\,2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=2v\\,z=-u} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+(2v)^{2}+(-u)^{2} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+4{v}^{2}+{u}^{2} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}+4{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(2{u}^{2}+4{v}^{2})\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate this integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+8\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v+8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v+8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172188 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
